cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Sixth Buddhist Council Tipiṭaka (Chaṭṭhasaṅgītipiṭaka) — Unicode corpus"
message: "If you use this corpus, please cite it using these metadata."
type: dataset
abstract: >-
  A searchable, cross-referenced Unicode corpus of the Sixth Buddhist Council recension of the
  Pāḷi Tipiṭaka with its Aṭṭhakathā (commentaries) and Ṭīkā (subcommentaries), 118 volumes and
  89,512 paragraphs, converted from the legacy-font edition published by the Ministry of
  Religious Affairs, Yangon. Includes structural extraction anchored to the printed page,
  54,036 variant readings and 27,153 cross-references transcribed from the printed footnote
  apparatus, and cross-layer paragraph linking.
authors:
  - name: "Instituto de Estudios Buddhistas Hispano (IEBH)"
  - name: "Buddhismo Theravāda Hispano A.R. (BTHAR)"
keywords:
  - Pāḷi
  - Tipiṭaka
  - Theravāda
  - Aṭṭhakathā
  - Ṭīkā
  - Chaṭṭhasaṅgīti
  - digital humanities
url: "https://buddha-dhamma.net"
repository-code: "https://github.com/bthar-mx/OSBCT"
license: CC-BY-4.0
version: "2.11.0"
date-released: "2026-10-21"
# 2026-10-21, v2.11.0: no text changed — the reader's word pane, and plain-text
# pages.  Corpus figures identical to 2.10.1.  Word pane: the Abhidhāna tab from
# IEBH's edition (abhidhana.buddha-dhamma.net), first; links into the IEBH
# glossary of grammatical terms and the Saddanīti roots (site/reader/
# gram-index.json); DPD entries by headword, the clicked form marked in DPD's
# own declension table; the clicked word also selected (⌘C, Padakosa ⌃⌥P); a
# sandhi line for the 363,254 corpus forms with no DPD headword (IEBH solver
# readings for 33,030, else DPD's split labelled as DPD's, unverified); and a
# Sandhi button running the IEBH solver — gramaticas.buddha-dhamma.net's engine
# copied unmodified (site/reader/sandhi-solver.js, _panel/build_solver.py) —
# on the clicked word.  The sandhi data and the solver's lexicon sit in the
# evaluation store, stores/lookup_eval/, because both were built with DPD.
# Also: the Dīghanikāya as 34 plain HTML sutta pages with commentary links
# (site/t/dn/), robots.txt, sitemaps, descriptions and canonicals, and
# pipeline/retitle_pdfs.py.  Minor: new reader features, the 2.9.0/2.10.0
# precedent.  Three-file checklist bumped together, tag cut LAST.
# 2026-09-09, v2.10.1: ONE WORD of the text changes, and the fault is the
# printed edition's own — the first such applied to the served text.  25KhuA06
# printed p. 58 prints "hoti. A yaṁ tāvetthapadato" with a space inside Ayaṁ
# at a line end; the original text layer has the gap too, so this is a
# misprint of the edition, not of the conversion.  Reported by the reader,
# verified at the printed page first, recorded as E078 with the printed
# reading preserved and the emendation attributed, and applied at the
# reader's decision by pipeline/apply_text_errata.py from the register:
# corpus paragraph, verse store, page-break map (re-derived: Δ-1, two later
# breaks moved by one), search index and postings, three frequency counters
# (stores on R2: r2_upload.sh + WLV 20260909a).  Nothing else moved.  Patch,
# not minor: the 2.7.1 precedent.  Three-file checklist bumped together, tag
# cut LAST.
# 2026-09-06, v2.10.0: no text changed — A PHRASE IS CONSECUTIVE WORDS.  A phrase
# had been counted as a substring of the paragraph text: `tassa bhagavato`
# inside *etassa bhagavato* (63 paragraphs), and *dhammā”ti* refused for
# `dhammā ti` because the edition closes the quotative up against its word.
# Now consecutive tokens, each word as the single-word search matches it;
# every phrase result line names the rule.  Counts move both ways (437/318
# for tassa bhagavato, was 527/379); pipeline/measure_phrase_semantics.py
# computes both rules.  A position store was measured and NOT built.  Also:
# the sweep reads one n-gram shard (tg/) instead of the 12.5 MB key list, and
# the section names one shard (tn/) instead of the 1.09 MB names.json; the
# largest file a search fetches is 514 KB, gated.  Every new check_search.js
# assertion failed on the previous build first.  Three-file checklist bumped
# together, tag cut LAST.
# 2026-09-05, v2.9.0: no text changed — search matches diacritics EXACTLY, and
# stops downloading the canon.  Every search key had been folded (tassā stored
# as tassa), so a search for either word counted both — 36,644 against the
# 4,322 that are tassā; 34,134 folded keys (5.3%) were merging printed forms.
# The index now stores the printed tokens; folding is the "Ignore diacritics"
# switch, off by default, and every result line names its mode.  The search
# no longer fetches per-volume files (117 of them, ~40 MB, for one common
# word): postings shards + text chunks, 2.3 MB for the same search, one
# implementation (site/searchcore.js) for both pages, pipeline/perf_search.js
# as the performance gate with the before and after recorded.  Dictionary
# panel: six round trips -> four, and the R2 origin is now edge-cached.
# check_search.js was made to FAIL on the previous build first.  Three-file
# checklist bumped together, tag cut LAST.
# 2026-08-31: the AUTHOR NAME was wrong, and had been wrong in every deposit.
# This file, `.zenodo.json`, the README and `LICENSE.md` all named "Buddhismo
# Theravāda México-Hispano AR".  The entity recognised by SEGOB (Secretaría de
# Gobernación) as an Asociación Religiosa is "Buddhismo Theravāda Hispano
# A.R." — without México — and that is the name under which the GitHub
# organisation `bthar-mx` is held.  Corrected in all four files.
# NO VERSION BUMP and no tag: the text, the corpus figures and every DOI are
# untouched, so the three-file checklist does not apply here.
# !!! THE TWELVE EXISTING ZENODO RECORDS STILL CARRY THE OLD NAME.  Only
# `.zenodo.json` reaches a deposit, and only deposits minted after this commit.
# v1.0.0 through v2.8.0 credit an entity under a name it is not registered
# under; each can be corrected in place on the record, which does not affect
# its DOI (as was done for v2.4.0, see the note below).
# 2026-08-31: THE README CLAIMED THE PĀḶI TEXT WAS PUBLIC DOMAIN.  It is not.
# `LICENSE.md` has always stated the position correctly — the edition's notice
# permits duplication for free, NON-COMMERCIAL distribution — and the README
# contradicted both that file and its own next sentence.  The same overstated
# claim was live on the site (`site/about.html`, ab_source2, both languages)
# and is corrected there too.  The corpus figures and DOIs are unaffected.
# 2026-08-16, v2.8.0: no text changed — the niggahita becomes the reader's
# choice.  The reader and the search page gain a toggle between the edition's
# ṁ and the modern ṃ.  Display-only and bijective: the stored corpus carries
# the edition's ṁ exclusively (verified — no ṃ in any served volume,
# apparatus, link or index file), so the swap runs over the screen, never the
# data, and switching back restores the display byte-exact.  Search matched
# both conventions already; the dictionary panel folds a clicked or typed
# word back to the edition's form before lookup.  Two same-day corrections
# are in the release and stated rather than hidden: the toggle's own tooltip
# was being rewritten by the very transform it explains (marked exempt), and
# the first exemption froze the button's label (the marker moved off the
# button).  Regression suite _niggtest.js, 24 cases, runs the shipped code.
# Three-file checklist bumped together, tag cut LAST.
# 2026-08-09, v2.7.1: ONE WORD of the text changes, and it is the first time
# since v2.3.0 that any reading has moved — 25VsmT01 `pathavīkasiṇādivaḍḍhanī`
# becomes `…vaḍḍhane`, settled by the reader against the printed page, one
# character for one character, so pbreak re-derived BYTE-EQUAL and the corpus
# figures in the abstract stand unchanged.  The rest completes what v2.7.0 left
# open: 41 register entries confirmed (49 of 68), eleven wrong suggestions
# corrected with their superseded readings kept, and NINE review-sheet sites
# WITHDRAWN — the PDFs read correctly at every one and the corrupt forms lived
# only in a gitignored snapshot three weeks stale.  Three-file checklist bumped
# together, tag cut LAST.
# 2026-08-09, v2.7.0: no text changed — the errata register under scrutiny.
# E021 resolved AGAINST both guesses: the printed x at 10Ma02 p.247 is the
# edition's own position siglum (paired with the + below it, one unnumbered
# foot-note); nothing applied, two superseded suggestions recorded in the
# entry.  The glyph review sheet (pipeline/build_glyph_review.py) renders
# all 50 unresolved census-glyph sites beside their printed pages; the
# reader's verdicts on 49 are recorded in _review/ — 37 confirm readings the
# served text already carries, 12 await per-row application.  The siglum
# measure (pipeline/measure_sigla.py): 1,427 of 2,535 symbol-marked
# paragraphs carry no apparatus entry at all.  Three-file checklist bumped
# together, tag cut LAST.
# 2026-08-08, v2.6.0: no text changed — 118 volumes, 89,512 paragraphs,
# RE-COUNTED and exact.  The release is the reader and the search again:
# layer-ordered results, the bucketed term index, the dictionary panel's
# gear, the DPD refresh (2026-07-28) with its family tables working for the
# first time, and the version said on every page.  All three checklist
# files bumped together, tag cut LAST.
# 2026-08-08, v2.5.0: no text changed in this release either.  The paragraph
# figure was RE-COUNTED by the method below and reproduces exactly: 118 volumes,
# 89,512 paragraphs.  Variants and cross-references are re-stated as at v2.4.0.
# (For the record: counting the `variants`/`xrefs` entries of the apparatus maps
# today gives 55,241 and 34,048 — each exactly ONE more than the figures the
# v2.3.0 note already records as not reproducible by that method.  The
# discrepancy predates this release and is stated, not resolved.)
# The release is the reader and the search: the printed footnote apparatus
# reaches the page, and both search UIs answer multi-word, phrase and wildcard
# queries.  Both metadata files bumped together, tag cut LAST, as below.
# !!! BUMPING THIS FILE ALONE DOES NOTHING TO THE DEPOSIT. `.zenodo.json` EXISTS
# IN THE REPOSITORY ROOT, AND WHEN IT DOES, ZENODO IGNORES THIS FILE ENTIRELY --
# not "prefers", ignores. Zenodo's own documentation says so.
#
# So the release checklist is BOTH files, every time — and since 2026-08-09
# a THIRD, because the site now says its version on every page:
#     .zenodo.json  -> "version", and the "New in x.y.z" paragraph of "description"
#     CITATION.cff  -> "version", "date-released", and the new DOI once minted
#     site/i18n.js  -> window.OSBCT_VERSION (painted into every .sitever element)
#
# Found on 2026-08-07 with the v2.4.0 tag not yet cut: this file had been bumped
# to 2.4.0 and `.zenodo.json` still said 2.3.0, so the deposit would have been
# minted describing the previous release while this file described the current
# one. That is the THIRD time this project's citation metadata has been a
# release behind -- see the two notes below -- and the first time the cause was
# that the wrong file was being maintained.
#
# !!! AND IT WAS CAUGHT TOO LATE. The v2.4.0 tag had already been cut at the
# commit before the fix, so the DEPOSITED tarball declares 2.3.0 in
# `.zenodo.json` while declaring 2.4.0 here. The Zenodo record's metadata was
# corrected in place (which does not affect the DOI); the archived files could
# not be. Recorded, not hidden: `docs/DEPOSIT_ERRATA.md`.
#
# The ordering that would have prevented it: CUT THE TAG LAST, after every
# metadata file is committed and pushed -- not between two of them.
# 2026-08-07, v2.4.0: the corpus figures in the abstract are DELIBERATELY
# unchanged. No text changed in that release -- it moved the dictionary stores
# out of site/ to a bucket and gave the reader a fallback to the copy inside
# this deposit. 118 volumes, 89,512 paragraphs, 54,036 variants and 27,153
# cross-references are the same numbers, re-stated rather than re-counted, and
# that is the correct treatment: a citation of v2.3.0 and a citation of v2.4.0
# name the same text.
# !!! THIS FILE WAS A RELEASE BEHIND.  It declared 2.1.0 while the repository was
# tagged v2.2.0 (2026-08-02), so every citation named a superseded version — the
# same failure the DOI note below records, one field over.  Bumping `version`
# and `date-released` is part of tagging, not an afterthought.
#
# THE FIGURES ABOVE ARE COUNTED, NOT CARRIED FORWARD.  Method, so they can be
# re-derived: paragraphs are the `paragraphs` arrays of the 118 `site/<VOL>.json`;
# variants and cross-references are the `variants` and `xrefs` entries of the
# per-volume maps in `site/reader/apparatus/`.  The previous deposit described
# 86,365 paragraphs, 55,240 variants and 34,047 cross-references; the paragraph
# figure moved because four Khuddaka commentaries were re-segmented, and the
# other two could not be reproduced by this method and are stated as measured
# rather than repeated.
# THE CONCEPT DOI, which always resolves to the newest deposited version.
# It used to name 10.5281/zenodo.21495339 — the version DOI of v1.0.0, dated
# 22 July — while this file declared version 2.0.0, so every citation sent the
# reader to a superseded archive.
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21495338"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21495338"
    description: "Zenodo concept DOI — all versions; resolves to the latest"
  # !!! EVERY DEPOSIT IS LISTED HERE, and the list is now complete.  v2.2.0 was
  # missing from this file for four days -- not because it was never deposited,
  # but because nothing added the line.  It exists: 10.5281/zenodo.21760727.
  # Checked against the Zenodo record's own Versions panel, 2026-08-06, which is
  # also where the concept DOI above was confirmed as `Cite all versions`.
  # Minted 2026-09-06 and READ FROM THE RECORD (zenodo.org/records/22527597),
  # not guessed: version 2.10.0, publication date 2026-09-06, the "New in
  # 2.10.0" paragraph present at minting — the phrase rule (consecutive
  # words), the tg/ and tn/ shards, no text changed; deposit
  # bthar-mx/OSBCT-v2.10.0.zip, 451 MB (the 890 tn/ files are the growth).
  # SEVENTH CONSECUTIVE DEPOSIT to arrive describing itself correctly.  No
  # in-place correction needed.
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.22527597"
    description: "Zenodo archive of v2.10.0 (6 September 2026)"
  # Minted 2026-09-05 (Zenodo stamps it 2026-09-06 — its clock is UTC and the
